Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560 align:start position:19%
The models have reached a point where
they have surpassed my ability to really

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:09.120 align:start position:19%
understand what they're capable of. The
sense that I get from talking to people

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.960 align:start position:19%
that are more familiar with the space is
that the models are extremely good at

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.240 align:start position:19%
combining a lot of different areas of
mathematics. It's arguable that they're

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:21.720 align:start position:19%
not superhuman yet, but we're also
seeing rapid progress on that side. But

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.680 align:start position:19%
over time it will just become superhuman
in basically every respect. And I don't

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.160 align:start position:19%
know how long that transition period is
going to take. It could be It could be a

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.000 align:start position:19%
couple of years. It could be could be 10
years, but I think we'll get there

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.000 align:start position:19%
eventually.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.160 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Welcome to the Game Theory.
I'm Slater with Bain Capital Ventures,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.560 align:start position:19%
and today we're talking with Noam Brown.
At OpenAI, Noam focuses on multi-agent

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.480 align:start position:19%
reasoning and test time compute scaling.
He was one of the key people behind O1,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.920 align:start position:19%
OpenAI's first public reasoning model.
Last year his team swept [music] the

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.440 align:start position:19%
math and CS contest circuit. Their model
won gold medals at the IMO, IOI, and

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.680 align:start position:19%
ICPC.
Today we'll talk about OpenAI's recent

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.360 align:start position:19%
disproof of the Erdős unit distance
conjecture and about AI for math

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.760 align:start position:19%
research in general.
Noam, thanks for coming today.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.840 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Of course. I'm great to be I'm happy to
be here.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.360 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Well, you know, we're so we're sitting
here today. It's about a week after

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.320 align:start position:19%
OpenAI announced
you sort of

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:17.520 align:start position:19%
disproof of this Erdős conjecture
on the planar unit distance problem. And

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.360 align:start position:19%
it's the first thing that I want to talk
about. And you know, maybe my first

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:26.120 align:start position:19%
question is why is this happening now?
You know, I think the IMO results, which

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.760 align:start position:19%
are already incredibly very incredibly
impressive, happened about 10 months

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.360 align:start position:19%
ago.
You know, sort of why are we seeing the

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:37.840 align:start position:19%
first sort of major results in math
research today as opposed to 10 months

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.080 align:start position:19%
ago? What's the different about the
models or the systems?

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:46.160 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; I mean, in many ways it's it's not that
surprising. When we got the IMO result

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.560 align:start position:19%
about a year ago, we expected You know,
we said that progress is going to

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.840 align:start position:19%
continue, that we'll see um
more significant results in math. And

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:59.440 align:start position:19%
eventually we're going to reach a point
where the models are proving things that

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:02.440 align:start position:19%
humans can't prove. And there were some
initial like signs like this over the

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.120 align:start position:19%
over the course of the year when the
model started proving things that were

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.240 align:start position:19%
unproven. Um, I think there were
questions around like, well, was that

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:14.120 align:start position:19%
just because humans hadn't paid much
attention to it or was it doing things

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:18.040 align:start position:19%
that were just like, you know, not that
interesting. And um,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:22.880 align:start position:19%
I think what's cool about this result is
like it's it's perhaps the first case of

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.720 align:start position:19%
like a model proving something that was
like really interesting to

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.959 align:start position:19%
mathematicians and also a very
significant problem. And we we kind of

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:35.120 align:start position:19%
felt like this was going to happen
eventually and it just, you know, the

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.600 align:start position:19%
models kept becoming more capable and
um,

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.360 align:start position:19%
it just finally happened. And I should
say this wasn't like,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.440 align:start position:19%
you know, it wasn't like me or my team
that like enabled this result. It's just

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.040 align:start position:19%
a natural consequence of the models
becoming more capable.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.760 align:start position:19%
Um, and then, you know, some researchers
at OpenAI decided to like, okay, well,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.160 align:start position:19%
is this at now at the level where it can
actually prove a problem like this? And

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.240 align:start position:19%
they ran it on that problem and it
turned out the answer was yes.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.560 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Were you um, one thing I wanted to ask

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.239 align:start position:19%
about is sort of like domains in math
that you think are you're sort of most

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.959 align:start position:19%
optimistic about? Do you feel like
combinatorics is an area where, you

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.519 align:start position:19%
know, you were especially interested or
you felt like rapid progress um, with AI

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.720 align:start position:19%
was sort of more possible or, you know,
sort of is it that behind the scenes

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.360 align:start position:19%
you're sort of trying lots and lots of
open problems and this just happened to

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.040 align:start position:19%
be the one
um, that fell first?

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.120 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't think there's anything um,
special about combinatorics. I don't

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.200 align:start position:19%
think I mean, basically I'm a result of
anything I was like a little bit bearish

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.280 align:start position:19%
on combinatorics.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.519 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; But um,
it it just it just so happened that

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.720 align:start position:19%
yeah, we just tried a bunch of different
problems and this one the answer came

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.360 align:start position:19%
back like, oh yeah, it seems like this
one's correct.

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:38.080 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Well, one thing I was interested in is

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:42.840 align:start position:19%
um, you know, the construction itself
and it looks it's it's very interesting

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.320 align:start position:19%
because it's the kind of thing that I
think a human mathematician would have

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:50.600 align:start position:19%
really struggled um, to come up with.
Um, you know, it's a very complicated

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:55.200 align:start position:19%
construction. It has like some analogies
to the original um, Erdos argument, but

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.320 align:start position:19%
it's it's quite a lot more complex. And
you know, you guys released this kind of

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:03.680 align:start position:19%
companion piece with commentary from
mathematicians on the results. And

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:08.600 align:start position:19%
you know, I think it was Jacob Zimmerman
who had remarks of like, man, you know,

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:12.080 align:start position:19%
I tried something like this a while ago,
but it just was sort of too complicated.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.400 align:start position:19%
You know, sort of the waters were too
treacherous, I think was his phrase. And

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.880 align:start position:19%
it sort of raises this interesting
question about whether

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.440 align:start position:19%
AI systems are going to be better at
math in ways that are really different

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.720 align:start position:19%
than humans. Like they sort of might be
maxed out in different dimensions.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.280 align:start position:19%
In other words, you know, in the
specific case, like maybe

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:35.840 align:start position:19%
even a human mathematician given the
prompt of try to find a counter example

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:39.120 align:start position:19%
to this might never come across this.
And I am curious for your commentary on

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.360 align:start position:19%
that. Do you kind of feel as though the
models are better at math in a way

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:47.360 align:start position:19%
that's different from humans as opposed
to saying being like a human, but just,

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.280 align:start position:19%
you know, some factor some factor
better.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.680 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; You know, it's interesting like the
models have reached a point where they

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.640 align:start position:19%
have surpassed my ability to really
understand

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:59.440 align:start position:19%
what they're capable of and like what
they excel at just because like already

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.280 align:start position:19%
the you know, these kinds of problems
like understanding the proofs so is just

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.120 align:start position:19%
beyond my ability. I'm sure it's beyond
most people's ability, but it's also

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:09.600 align:start position:19%
beyond my ability. And
so it's hard for me at this point to say

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.960 align:start position:19%
like, oh yeah, these are like the weak
points of the models and this these are

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.560 align:start position:19%
like the strong points of the models in
the field of math.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.320 align:start position:19%
But I
the sense that I get from talking to

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.160 align:start position:19%
people that are more familiar with the
space

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:24.720 align:start position:19%
both at OpenAI and external
mathematicians is that the models are

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:28.720 align:start position:19%
extremely good at combining a lot of
different areas of mathematics.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.840 align:start position:19%
And they're also like in this in this
respect I would say that they're

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.120 align:start position:19%
superhuman at this point.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:38.919 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; And then there's the separate question
of like reasoning through the the the

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:43.400 align:start position:19%
complex problems. And this I think like
the argument it's arguable that they're

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.440 align:start position:19%
not superhuman yet, but we're also
seeing rapid progress on this. And so I

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.640 align:start position:19%
think we're going to see this kind of
spiky intelligence where

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:55.120 align:start position:19%
um
the models are going to be superhuman in

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:58.800 align:start position:19%
some respects and then also not quite
superhuman in other respects and the

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:02.080 align:start position:19%
point but over time as the models become
more capable they are going to become

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.560 align:start position:19%
like more capable across the board and
that's what we're seeing. It's that it's

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.040 align:start position:19%
not just like oh they're getting better
and better at ingesting a lot of

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.080 align:start position:19%
mathematics and combining it. It's that
they're getting better at that but

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.600 align:start position:19%
they're also getting better at just like
the fundamental reasoning capabilities.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:18.320 align:start position:19%
So
I

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:21.360 align:start position:19%
I think we will in the short term see
more of these more more results where

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.600 align:start position:19%
it's like
combining different areas of mathematics

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:28.440 align:start position:19%
and in novel ways that that nobody
thought to do before and then

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.000 align:start position:19%
having it be like really cool proofs in
this kind of way

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:36.040 align:start position:19%
but over time it will just become
superhuman in basically every respect.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.160 align:start position:19%
And I don't know how long that
transition period is going to take. It

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.320 align:start position:19%
could be it could be a couple of years,
it could be could be 10 years

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.480 align:start position:19%
um
but I think we'll get there eventually.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.320 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah. What do you That is one thing I
was curious about is if you speculate

00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:52.400 align:start position:19%
that there is this kind of like centaur
period for sort of like human

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:56.560 align:start position:19%
aided math or you know if you think that
that period is too short to be sort of

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:58.880 align:start position:19%
interesting in the long run. Curious
what you think about that.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.760 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think there there definitely
will be a centaur period and this

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.240 align:start position:19%
the classic analogy which I think is
appropriate is to things like chess and

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:11.919 align:start position:19%
go where in chess
you have Garry Kasparov losing to Deep

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:17.480 align:start position:19%
Blue in like 1997 and then there is this
like 10-year period where

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:22.200 align:start position:19%
okay chess AIs were better in some ways
but you know they weren't like the human

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.400 align:start position:19%
plus the AI was actually better than the
human alone or or an AI alone.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:29.160 align:start position:19%
And at this point the AIs are so good at
chess that like

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:33.560 align:start position:19%
the human doesn't really add anything
and so it's like clear that it is beyond

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:37.240 align:start position:19%
the human's ability to add anything to
the to the table. Um but

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.560 align:start position:19%
and and I think we'll see something
similar with mathematics where okay the

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:45.560 align:start position:19%
AIs are like extremely effective in some
ways but they're not and and we might

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:47.720 align:start position:19%
reach a point where they're just like if
you had a choice between a human

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.320 align:start position:19%
mathematician or an AI mathematician,
you would choose the AI mathematician.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:55.400 align:start position:19%
But even when we reach that point, you
could argue that the human plus the AI

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.240 align:start position:19%
would be more effective than either
alone.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.880 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Now, how long that period is, it's hard

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:04.240 align:start position:19%
to say. It it
it could be very short, it could be it

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.840 align:start position:19%
could be long. I I think a lot of it
depends on

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:11.520 align:start position:19%
uh on the trajectory of mathematical
progress. I think also this is where the

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:15.000 align:start position:19%
analogy to things like chess and Go
break down because chess and Go became

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:19.480 align:start position:19%
superhuman because of self-play and the
ability of the AI to just like

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:23.520 align:start position:19%
get collect infinite data and like play
against itself and get arbitrarily

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:26.560 align:start position:19%
better. And we don't really have a
similar version of self-play in

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:30.640 align:start position:19%
mathematics because it's not a
two-player zero-sum game.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:34.479 align:start position:19%
So,
you it could take longer for the

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:37.320 align:start position:19%
capabilities to improve. Now, that said,
I mean also the capabilities have been

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.719 align:start position:19%
improving very rapidly and so I don't
know, it's a trade it's like kind of a

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:46.440 align:start position:19%
trade-off. It's kind of I'm trying to
balance the fact that like AI progress

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:50.440 align:start position:19%
has been extremely fast so far um versus
the fact that like there is the

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:54.520 align:start position:19%
possibility that
progress slows down as you reach

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.240 align:start position:19%
superhuman capabilities.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've um you would know much more

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.440 align:start position:19%
than me, but I sort of heard like
speculations around the self-play idea

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.560 align:start position:19%
of like, you know, you should have
someone propose one model proposes

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:08.520 align:start position:19%
problems, the other tries to solve them.
You can kind of adversarially make the

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:11.680 align:start position:19%
problems more difficult in some way, but
maybe it's it's like very hard to get to

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:16.000 align:start position:19%
work. It's it seems way clear less clear
how you would do that than in like a

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:19.240 align:start position:19%
like a sort of very um sort of boxed
setting like a like playing Go.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:24.760 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is like
I think people under appreciate how

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:28.080 align:start position:19%
unique two-player zero-sum games are
when it comes to self-play. Like in in

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:31.000 align:start position:19%
these two-player zero-sum games, you
have a very clear objective, which is

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:36.200 align:start position:19%
you want to converge to a minimax
policy. And you can do like you just

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.520 align:start position:19%
have the AIs play against each other.
You don't need any external data. You

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.400 align:start position:19%
don't need any human data.
Just have the AIs play against each

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.840 align:start position:19%
other and figure out how to beat each
other and then learn from those mistakes

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:49.000 align:start position:19%
and they will converge to the minimax
policy.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:53.200 align:start position:19%
When you go outside of a two-player
zero-sum game, you don't have this

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.320 align:start position:19%
property anymore. So, an example I like
to give is the ultimatum game. Are you

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.840 align:start position:19%
familiar with the ultimatum game?
&gt;&gt; No.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:05.320 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Okay, so the ultimatum game, it's a
non-two-player zero-sum game.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:08.800 align:start position:19%
One player, Alice, has $100 and they
have to choose how much to give to Bob.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.760 align:start position:19%
They can offer anywhere between $0 and
$100.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.360 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Mhm.
&gt;&gt; And Bob chooses to reject or accept.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.280 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Mhm.
&gt;&gt; And if Bob rejects, then both players

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:20.400 align:start position:19%
receive $0. If Bob accepts, then the
money is split according to what Alice

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:22.400 align:start position:19%
proposed. So, like let's say Alice
offers 20 bucks.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:28.160 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Bob accepts, then Alice gets 80, Bob

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.040 align:start position:19%
gets 20. And then if Bob rejects, then
they both get zero.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:34.200 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Mhm.
&gt;&gt; And so,

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:37.240 align:start position:19%
you know, you could imagine what
self-play would converge to in this

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.920 align:start position:19%
game. If you had to guess, like what
what do you think it would converge to?

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.640 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Like always accepting.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, always accepting and then like

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.000 align:start position:19%
Alice offering basically like a dollar
or something or a penny.

00:10:45.839 --> 00:10:51.480 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; If you were to do this with humans, it

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:54.280 align:start position:19%
would not go very well, right? Like if I
were to offer you like, okay, I have 100

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.280 align:start position:19%
bucks, I'm going to offer you a penny.
You have a choice of accepting or

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.160 align:start position:19%
rejecting. Like
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.720 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; how would you react?
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah, I would

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.800 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; So,
so like once you go outside of

